void represents functions that complete execution but don’t return a value, typically used for side-effect functions like logging or API calls.
never represents functions that never complete, such as those that throw errors or run infinitely. It’s also used in exhaustive checks to ensure all cases are handled


✅ What is void?

👉 void means:
“This function does not return anything meaningful.”

✅ Example
function logMessage(): void {
  console.log("Hello");
}

👉 This function:
runs successfully
but returns nothing useful
  
🧠 Important Detail
Even though JS returns undefined, in TS:
👉 void ≠ exactly undefined
👉 It means ignore the return value

⚠️ What happens if you return something?
function logMessage(): void {
  return "hello"; // ❌ error
}
👉 Not allowed (in strict typing)

✅ Where void is used
1. Event handlers (React)
const handleClick = (): void => {
  console.log("clicked");
};
2. Functions with side effects
function saveData(): void {
  // API call
}
👉 No return, just action

  
🔥 2. never in TypeScript
✅ What is never?
👉 never means:
“This function never finishes normally.”

✅ Example 1: Throwing error
function throwError(): never {
  throw new Error("Something went wrong");
}

👉 Function:
never returns
execution stops
✅ Example 2: Infinite loop
function infiniteLoop(): never {
  while (true) {}
}

👉 Function:
never ends
never returns
    
🧠 Key Insight
👉 void → function completes, returns nothing
👉 never → function never completes

🔥 3. Real Difference (CRITICAL)
Feature	void	never
Returns	Nothing (undefined)	Never returns
Execution	Completes	Never completes
Use case	Side effects	Errors / impossible states

🔥 4. Practical Use of never (IMPORTANT)
✅ Exhaustive Checks (Senior-level concept)
type Shape = "circle" | "square";

function getArea(shape: Shape) {
  if (shape === "circle") return 1;
  if (shape === "square") return 2;

  const _exhaustiveCheck: never = shape; // 🔥 important
}

👉 If you add "triangle" later → TypeScript throws error
  type Shape = "circle" | "square" | "triangle";
Now your function is:

function getArea(shape: Shape) {
  if (shape === "circle") return 1;
  if (shape === "square") return 2;

  const _exhaustiveCheck: never = shape;
}
  What happens now?

👉 TypeScript thinks:

"circle" handled ✅
"square" handled ✅
"triangle" ❌ NOT handled

So now:

👉 shape = "triangle"
  ❌ Now this line breaks:
const _exhaustiveCheck: never = shape;

👉 Error:

Type '"triangle"' is not assignable to type 'never'
💥 WHY this is powerful

👉 It forces you to handle ALL cases

✅ Fix (add missing case)
if (shape === "triangle") return 3;

Now again:
👉 All cases handled → shape = never → no error

  🎯 Why companies love this pattern

Because:

Prevents bugs when new cases are added
Forces developers to update logic
Used in reducers, APIs, UI states
🚀 Real-world React Example
type Status = "loading" | "success" | "error";

function renderUI(status: Status) {
  if (status === "loading") return "Loading...";
  if (status === "success") return "Data loaded";
  if (status === "error") return "Error occurred";

  const _check: never = status;
}

👉 If someone adds "idle" later → TS forces update
Why this matters:
👉 Helps catch missing cases at compile time

🔥 5. void vs never in Assignments
let a: void;
let b: never;

a = undefined; // ✅
b = undefined; // ❌

👉 never is stricter

🔥 6. Function Inference Difference
Function declaration
function test() {
  throw new Error();
}
👉 inferred as never
  
Function expression
const test = () => {
  throw new Error();
};
👉 also never

🔥 7. Common Mistakes
❌ Confusing void with undefined
let x: void = undefined; // ✅ but not recommended
❌ Using never incorrectly
function test(): never {
  return; // ❌ wrong
}

👉 never must NOT return


⚡ Simple Analogy
void → “I finish but give nothing”
never → “I never finish”
  
🚀 Pro Insight (Senior Level)
Use void for side-effect functions
Use never for:
error handling
exhaustive checks
impossible states
